0.3.3 - 2026-11-07
------------------

  github secret scanning opened "Google API Key, public leak"
  against src/yt_search.py line 10.

  what it actually was

    * AIzaSy...11qcW8 is youtube's own public web client
      InnerTube key. It is embedded in the html that
      youtube.com serves to every anonymous visitor, verified
      by curling the home page and finding the identical value.
      It is not this account's credential, carries no quota
      billed to anyone here, and nothing was exposed. The
      scanner matches the AIzaSy shape and cannot tell a public
      client key from a private one.

  removed anyway, for a better reason

    * hardcoding it meant breakage if google ever rotated it.
      And the clientVersion sitting next to it was already
      stale: the code said 2.20261001.00.00 while youtube was
      serving 2.20261030.02.00. That is a real latent bug, not
      a security one.
    * ys_conf() now reads INNERTUBE_API_KEY and
      INNERTUBE_CLIENT_VERSION out of the youtube home page on
      first use and caches both for 6 hours. One extra request
      per 6 hours, and the client version can no longer go
      stale.
    * if that read fails the search falls back to the html
      ytInitialData scrape, which needs no key at all, so this
      did not add a single point of failure.
    * check.sh now fails if any tracked file carries an
      AIza shaped literal, so this cannot come back.

  on the alert itself

    * the value stays in git history. Rewriting public history
      is not warranted for a string youtube publishes to the
      world. Dismiss the alert as a false positive.

  checked by hand

    * curling youtube.com returns the same key the code used,
      proving it is public
    * live search works with the key fetched at runtime, picks
      up client 2.20261030.02.00 automatically
    * the config is read once and reused on the next search
    * no AIza literal left in the tree, and check.sh now
      catches one if it reappears


